# Clinical Rationale
## Digital Mental Health NLP Pipeline for Asian Adolescents


## 1. Why Focus on Asian Adolescents?

### 1.1 Epidemiological Justification

Singapore's 2024 National Mental Health Survey found 1 in 4 young adults (18–29) reporting poor mental health — the highest rate across all age groups (MOH, 2024). The same crisis exists across APAC wherever three conditions intersect:

- **High-stakes academic systems** — PSLE/O-Levels (Singapore), GSAT (Taiwan), Suneung (South Korea), university entrance exams (Japan)
- **Collectivist cultural values** — face preservation, family obligation, shame avoidance reduce voluntary help-seeking
- **Mental health stigma** — 88.7% of Singapore young adults prefer online over face-to-face help-seeking (MOH, 2024)

### 1.2 Cultural Expression of Distress

| Expression Type | Western Pattern | Asian Adolescent Pattern |
|---|---|---|
| Direct disclosure | "I feel depressed" | Rare — face-saving concerns |
| Somatic framing | Treated as physical complaint | Primary distress proxy: "头很痛，睡不着" |
| Academic framing | Separate from mental health | Core distress marker: "考试又失败了" |
| Indirect language | Underused | Primary mode: "最近好累" |
| Family reference | Individual focus | Central: "让父母失望" |
| Help-seeking | Direct requests | Embedded in complaints, rarely explicit |
| Code-switching | Uncommon | Common: "好stressed, cannot tahan anymore lah" |

### 1.3 Digital Determinants of Health

Digital access is not the same as digital benefit (Chee, 2026). The same digital service produces a different patient reality depending on:

| Digital Determinant | Pipeline Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| Device access | No smartphone = invisible to system | Complement with school self-report surveys |
| Internet connectivity | Unstable connections affect data completeness | Offline fallback options |
| Digital literacy | Low confidence users less active online | Multi-channel data collection |
| Digital trust | Fear of data misuse reduces consent rates | Transparent consent + privacy-by-design |
| Algorithmic fairness | Absent from training data = invisible to care | Stratified validation by demographics |

> "The people most absent from the data may become the people most invisible to digital healthcare." (Chee, 2026)

