## 2. Why NLP-Based Screening?

### 2.1 The Help-Seeking Gap

88.7% of Singapore young adults prefer online over face-to-face help-seeking (MOH, 2024). Current digital mental health platforms are **reactive** — they serve people who seek them out. Most at-risk adolescents never initiate contact.

The NLP solution: passively monitor digital text where adolescents already express distress naturally — identifying at-risk individuals before they reach crisis.

### 2.2 Asset-Based Approach (ABCD)

Following Asset-Based Community Development principles (Chee, 2026), this pipeline is designed to **enhance existing community mental health assets** — not replace them:

- School counsellors remain the primary care relationship
- Existing crisis services are the action pathways — the pipeline routes to them, not around them
- Peer support networks are a medium-risk output option
- Community trust is built through transparency and opt-in consent

People are producers, not just consumers of services. The pipeline empowers counsellors with better information — it does not replace their clinical judgment.

### 2.3 Advantages Over Traditional Screening

| Approach | Coverage | Frequency | Stigma Barrier |
|---|---|---|---|
| Clinical interview | Low | Occasional | High |
| Self-report surveys | Moderate | Termly | Moderate |
| Teacher referral | Low | Ad hoc | High dependency |
| **NLP pipeline** | **High** | **Continuous** | **No barrier** |

