## 3. Clinical Rationale for 3-Tier Architecture

### 3.1 Why Three Tiers?

A single model forces an unacceptable clinical tradeoff:
- High sensitivity → excessive false positives → counsellor fatigue → system abandonment
- High specificity → missed at-risk individuals → clinical harm

The 3-tier architecture resolves this by applying progressively deeper analysis only to flagged cases.

### 3.2 Tier 1 — MentalBERT Rationale

Chinese MentalBERT was pre-trained on Chinese social media mental health text — the same distribution as the target population. Domain specificity is clinically critical:

- General Chinese BERT tokenises "绝望感" as separate characters, losing compound meaning
- MentalBERT preserves compound distress terms as semantic units
- Tuned for high sensitivity — missing a true at-risk individual at Tier 1 is a clinical error

### 3.3 Tier 2 — XGBoost + SHAP Rationale

XGBoost chosen over deep neural networks specifically for **clinical interpretability**. SHAP values provide feature-level explanations that:
- Enable clinical validation of the score
- Support counsellor conversation ("I notice you've been posting late at night this week...")
- Allow audit and accountability
- Build institutional trust

| Feature | Clinical Basis |
|---|---|
| Hopelessness language | Strongest predictor of suicide risk (Beck, 1974) |
| Social isolation signals | Key depression symptom; predicts escalation |
| Late-night posting (11pm–4am) | Disrupted sleep is both symptom and risk factor |
| Academic stress markers | Exam periods correlate with crisis presentations |
| Somatic complaints | Culturally normative distress proxy in Asian populations |
| Positive language (inverse) | Low positive affect predicts depression onset |
| Code-switching | Higher emotional arousal in bilingual speakers |

### 3.4 Tier 3 — LSTM Rationale

Grounded in Eckstein et al. (2026): human behaviour encodes risk history at multiple timescales. Point-in-time assessment misses trend direction, peak prediction, and cyclical patterns.

> Point-in-time: "Risk score = 65" → Counsellor uncertain when to act
> Trajectory: "Risk peaks at 78 in 2 days" → Counsellor acts today

