## 4. Avoiding "Technically Correct, Contextually Wrong"

A critical risk in any digital health deployment is building a system that is technically accurate but clinically inappropriate (Chee, 2026):

- Risk scores must use **clinical language** counsellors recognise — not data science terminology
- Feature explanations must reference **observable behaviours** ("posting at 3am three nights this week") not abstract features ("temporal_late_night = 0.87")
- Output interface must be tested with real counsellors before deployment
- Workflow must fit how counsellors already work — the system adapts to them, not the other way round

> "There is no best workflow, just effective workflow." (Chee, 2026)

---

## 5. Change Management Considerations

Three leading causes of digital health project failure (Chee, 2026):
1. Scope creep
2. Expectation management failure
3. System forces workflow to change instead of the other way round

This pipeline addresses all three:
- **Scope:** Clearly defined 3-tier architecture with documented limitations
- **Expectations:** Explicit statement that this is decision-support, not diagnosis
- **Workflow:** Co-design with counsellors required before deployment

